-------------------------------------------------------------------

     =========================================================
     Geant4 - an Object-Oriented Toolkit for Simulation in HEP
     =========================================================

                            G4SKspallation
                            -----------

 This simulation demonstrates muon spallation process in the SK Chrenkov water tank detector 
with simple conditions for the geometry.

	
 1- GEOMETRY DEFINITION
	
   The geometry is constructed in the G4SKDetectorConstruction class.
   The setup consists of a an envelope(world) of box shape containing two volumes: 
   the water tank cylinder and the fiducial volume of the tank which is also a cylinder.
   ***Fiducial volume is commented - if you want to measure things only for the fiducial volume 
   you have to uncomment lines in the detector construction.***

   
        |<---------- 42 m------------->|
        |                              |                       
        ================================ _ _
        ||water tank       	      ||   ^
        ||                   	      ||   |  
        ||  ========================  ||   |
        ||  |fiducial volume       |  ||   |
 muon   ||  |       	  	   |  ||   | 39 m
=======>||  |                      |  ||   |
        ||  |                	   |  ||   |
	||  ========================  ||   |
	||                     	      ||   v
	||                            || _ _
        ================================
    


   The envelope(world) is made of water (2.7 m water ~ 1 m rock) and the two inner volumes
   are also made of water.  

   The materials are created with the help of the G4NistManager class,
   which allows to build a material from the NIST database using their
   names. All available materials can be found in the Geant4 User's Guide
   for Application Developers, Appendix 10: Geant4 Materials Database.
		
 2- PHYSICS LIST
 
   The particle's type and the physics processes which will be available
   in this simulation are set in the QGSP_BERT_HP physics list, or in the QGSP_BIC_HP or
   in the FTFP_BERT_HP or in the QGSP_INCLXX_HP (for later comparison of results of these 
   different lists). These physics lists require data files for electromagnetic 
   and hadronic processes.
   See more on installation of the datasets in Geant4 Installation Guide,
   Chapter 3.3: Note On Geant4 Datasets:
   http://geant4.web.cern.ch/geant4/UserDocumentation/UsersGuides
                                           /InstallationGuide/html/ch03s03.html
   The following datasets: G4LEDATA, G4LEVELGAMMADATA, G4NEUTRONXSDATA,
   G4SAIDXSDATA and G4ENSDFSTATEDATA are mandatory for this simulation.

   In addition the build-in interactive command:
               /process/(in)activate processName
   allows to activate/inactivate the processes one by one.

   The physics list can be chosen by addind an extra argument when opening the application
   in batch mode. The third argument corresponds to the physics list choice. By default we 
   use the QGSP_BERT_HP. If the third argument is "0", we keep the QGSP_BERT_HP. If it is "1", we 
   get the QGSP_BIC_HP. "2" brings the FTFP_BERT_HP and "3" the QGSP_INCLXX_HP. Any other number or
   value for the third argument will bring the default physics list. If the argument is "!", it just
   keep the default one and you can add a fourth argument. Meaning that "!" is just a place holder. 
   
 3- ACTION INITALIZATION

   Two newly introduced classes, G4SKFixedActionInitialization and G4SKReadActionInitialization, 
   instantiates and registers to Geant4 kernel all user action classes.

   While in sequential mode the action classes are instatiated just once,
   via invoking the method:
      G4SKActionInitialization::Build() 
   in multi-threading mode the same method is invoked for each thread worker
   and so all user action classes are defined thread-local.

   A run action class is instantiated both thread-local 
   and global that's why its instance is created also in the method
      G4SKActionInitialization::BuildForMaster() 
   which is invoked only in multi-threading mode.
  	 
 4- PRIMARY GENERATOR
  
   The primary generator is defined in the G4SKPrimaryGeneratorAction class.
   The default kinematics is a 271 GeV muon(which is average muon energy for Super-Kamiokande) 
   with downward momentum along the cylinder axis located on top of the tank's water in the center
   of the tank surface.
   
   One may choose to read the initial conditions for the muons from an ASCII file. For this to be done,
   one have to write the initial conditions in the ini_cond.txt text file, keeping the same file format. 
   Then when lauching the application, if the command has a fourth argument the initial conditions will be 
   read from the file. 
   For example, I want to keep the default physics list, read the run2.mac macro and use the initial conditions
   from the ini_cond.txt file. So I use the following command :
   $  ./G4SKspallation run2.mac ! yes
   The last argument "yes" could be anything.

   This default setting can be changed via the Geant4 built-in commands of the G4ParticleGun class.
     
 5- DETECTOR RESPONSE

   This simulation contains two scoring methods. 

   One is command-based method implemented in the main function with the line : 
   G4ScoringManager * scManager = G4ScoringManager::GetScoringManager();  
   And then with scoring macros. A few different macros are available in the git hub repository.
	***This method is desactivated(commented for now)

   The other method is introduced with the sensitive detector construction. This is more complex
   and , for this specific simulation , requires the definition of some classes :
   SKRunAction, SKEventAction, SKHits, SKSD, SKAnalysis.
   And some changes in the action initialization and in the detector geometry classes.

   Alternative ways of scoring via Geant4 classes can be found in many Geant4 official examples.
   
   The energy deposited in the SK tank is collected step by step in the SKSD and accumulated event 
   by event in a SKHit class object. The tank being the sensitive detector defined (one can change 
   the sensitive detector only to the fiducial volume by a few lines in the SKDetectorConstruction 
   class).

   At end of an event, the value acummulated in a SKHit class object is added to a histogram in SKEventAction.

   The rest of the data is added to histograms and ntuples, which are created in the SKRunAction, already
   whithin the SKSD::ProcessHits(G4Step* step, G4TouchableHistory* history) virtual function. These 
   histograms and ntuples are all saved in a root file named "SK.root"(it can be changed to a csv or 
   a xml file in the SKAnalysis).

 A- VISUALISATION

   The visualization manager is set via the G4VisExecutive class
   in the main() function in G4SKspallation.cc.    
   The initialisation of the drawing is done via a set of /vis/ commands
   in the macro vis.mac. This macro is automatically read from
   the main function when the example is used in interactive running mode.

   By default, vis.mac opens an OpenGL viewer (/vis/open OGL).
   The user can change the initial viewer by commenting out this line
   and instead uncommenting one of the other /vis/open statements, such as
   HepRepFile or DAWNFILE (which produce files that can be viewed with the
   HepRApp and DAWN viewers, respectively).  Note that one can always
   open new viewers at any time from the command line.  For example, if
   you already have a view in, say, an OpenGL window with a name
   "viewer-0", then
      /vis/open DAWNFILE
   then to get the same view
      /vis/viewer/copyView viewer-0
   or to get the same view *plus* scene-modifications
      /vis/viewer/set/all viewer-0
   then to see the result
      /vis/viewer/flush

   The DAWNFILE, HepRepFile drivers are always available
   (since they require no external libraries), but the OGL driver requires
   that the Geant4 libraries have been built with the OpenGL option.

   The vis.mac macro in G4SKspallation has additional commands
   that demonstrate additional functionality of the vis system, such as
   displaying text, axes, scales, date, logo and shows how to change
   viewpoint and style.  To see even more commands use help or
   ls or browse the available UI commands in the Application
   Developers Guide, Section 7.1.

   For more information on visualization, including information on how to
   install and run DAWN, OpenGL and HepRApp, see the visualization tutorials,
   for example,
   http://geant4.slac.stanford.edu/Presentations/vis/G4[VIS]Tutorial/G4[VIS]Tutorial.html
   (where [VIS] can be replaced by DAWN, OpenGL and HepRApp)

   The tracks are automatically drawn at the end of each event, accumulated
   for all events and erased at the beginning of the next run.

 B- USER INTERFACES
 
   The user command interface is set via the G4UIExecutive class
   in the main() function in G4SKspallation.cc 
   The selection of the user command interface is then done automatically 
   according to the Geant4 configuration or it can be done explicitly via 
   the third argument of the G4UIExecutive constructor (see exampleB4a.cc of the Geant4-10.06.p01 examples 
   distributed with the Geant4 source). 
 
 C- HOW TO RUN

    - Execute G4SKspallation in the 'interactive mode' with visualization:
        % ./G4SKspallation
      and type in the commands line by line:  
        Idle> /control/verbose 2
        Idle> /tracking/verbose 1
        Idle> /run/beamOn 1
        Idle> ...
        Idle> exit
      or
        Idle> /control/execute run1.mac
        ....
        Idle> exit

    - Execute G4SKspallation in the 'batch' mode from macro files 
      (without visualization)
        % ./G4SKspallation run2.mac


End.
